Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 2   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Complex functions",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title",
  Evaluatable->False,
  PageBreakAbove->True,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["2 September 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Understanding Complex Functions",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Polynomials",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Explaining CartesianMap", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["The Problem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The difficulty of picturing the behavior of functions from ",
  StyleBox["Complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["Complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is that the complex numbers are naturally represented in ",
  StyleBox["2 dimensions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The graph of a complex function, then, has to be represented in ",
  StyleBox["4 dimensions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!  As that is impossible to do with ordinary graphics, we try to find some \
other methods of getting a feeling for a complex function as a transformation \
of the two-dimensional complex plane into itself."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["An Answer.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "   One easy tactic is to focus on a number of related points and then to \
see how they are transformed.  By taking a fairly large number of points in a \
representative part of the plane, we begin to see what the mapping is doing.  \
The primary method is that of a ",
  StyleBox["grid",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " which is taken to be  rectangular.  The creation of the transform of this \
grid (at least to a reasonable degree of approximation) is carried out by the \
program ",
  StyleBox["CartesianMap",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Information["\<CartesianMap\>", LongForm \[Rule] False]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Polar coordinates can also be used and are often helpful for \
visualization.  Use the command ",
  StyleBox["PolarMap",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "SmallText",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since the ",
  StyleBox["identity function",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a transformation that does nothing, we can then see by comparison what \
the grid to be transformed by some other function looks like."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[z_] := z; \n
    ii = CartesianMap[f, {0, 1, 0.1}, {0, 1, 0.1}, 
        PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 1.1}, {\(-0.1\), 1.1}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The next transformation that comes to mind is a Euclidean transformation \
of the form ",
  StyleBox[" z\[Rule]a+b z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[z_] := 1 + I + \((2 - I)\)\ z; \n
    tt = CartesianMap[f, {0, 1, 0.1}, {0, 1, 0.1}, 
        PlotRange \[Rule] {{0, 4}, {\(-0.1\), 3.2}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we see that the unit square has been translated, stretched, and \
rotated.  We can in fact show the original grid and the transformed grid \
together by the mapping   ",
  StyleBox["z\[Rule]1+I+(2-I) z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[{ii, tt}, PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 4}, {\(-0.1\), 3.2}}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that the factor of magnification of this transformation is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\@5\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Our next most favorite transformation is, of course, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z \[Rule] z\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1, 0.1}, {0, 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 1.1}, {\(-0.2\), 2.1}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we see the square has undergone a considerable ",
  StyleBox["distortion",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  "In fact, what happened to the original four corners?  We can check:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f/@{0, 1, 1 + I, I}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Problem: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "An interesting question is to figure out ",
  StyleBox["what curves",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we are seeing there as the images of the formerly straight grid lines."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Solution:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Take the vertical line at position ",
  StyleBox["1/2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis, for example.  In ",
  StyleBox["Complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " notation, it can be expressed as the parametric form ",
  StyleBox["1/2 + I t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", where ",
  StyleBox["t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an independent real variable.  Now, just substitute this for ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in the formula for the function."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[f[1\/2 + I\ t]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We should associate this as ",
  StyleBox["(1/4 - t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSize->10,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->"Superscript"}],
  StyleBox[") + I t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  We then recognize the curve as a parabola lying on its side and opening \
to the left.  Let us plot the line and its image curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{1\/4\ \((4 - t\^2)\) == x, t == y}, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{4\ x == 1 - 4\ y\^2, x == 1\/2}, \n
      \t{x, \(-4.1\), 1.1}, {y, \(-4.1\), 4.1}, \n\t
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This same method can be applied to the other lines of the original \
rectangular grid\[LongDash]or to ",
  StyleBox["any other line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the plane."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[f[1 - 2\ t + I\ \((1 + t)\)]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{1 - 2\ t == x, 1 + t == y}, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{3\ \((\(-2\)\ t + t\^2)\) == x, 
        \(-2\)\ \((\(-1\) + t + 2\ t\^2)\) == y}, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{x == 3 - 2\ y, 
        x\^2 + x\ \((\(-108\) + 24\ y)\) == 324 - 144\ y - 9\ y\^2}, {x, 
        \(-4.1\), 4.1}, {y, \(-4.1\), 4.1}, \n\tAxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exploring powers", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Let us look at other grids and other powers of z in the formula for \
the complex mapping.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[z_] := \((1 + I)\)\ z\^2; \n\n
    CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {0, 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2.5\), 2}, {\(-1.5\), 2.5}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
There we see the first squaring image followed by a rotation and \
change of scale.  Here is the image of a different rectangle.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, {0, 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 1.1}, {\(-2.1\), 2.1}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Now what happened to the corners?  And on to the cubes.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^3; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {0, 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-1\), 2.5}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^3; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {\(-1\), 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2.5\), 2.5}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Can you explain the overlap?  Note that now we are getting into ",
  StyleBox["cubic curves",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   To see them, let us look at one thin strip from the grid."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^3; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0.4, 0.5, 0.1}, {\(-1\), 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 0.5}, {\(-1\), 1}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["We can check one of these lines parametrically.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[f[1\/2 + I\ t]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{x == 1\/8 - \(3\ t\^2\)\/2, y == \(3\ t\)\/4 - t\^3}, t]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{x == 1\/2, 6\ x + 15\ x\^2 + 8\ x\^3 == 1 - 27\ y\^2}, {
        x, \(-4.1\), 4.1}, {y, \(-4.1\), 5.1}, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This turns out to be a well known type of  ",
  StyleBox["cubic curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", which we will study in detail in the latter part of this course.  By the \
way, why does the curve cross the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis at the point ",
  StyleBox["-1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ?"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
And, as we might expect, the higher the power, the more the square \
is twisted around.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^4; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {0, 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-4\), 1.1}, {\(-2\), 2}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^5; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {0, 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-4\), 2}, {\(-4\), 2}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^5; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-4\), 4}, {\(-4\), 4}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^6; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {0, 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-4\), 4}, {\(-8\), 2}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^6; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-4\), 4}, {\(-8\), 8}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Finally, a look at a higher-degree curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^7; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0.9, 1, 0.1}, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-5\), 11}, {\(-9\), 9}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Unfortunately, several minutes of computation could not find the formula \
for such a curve ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Elimination",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " from the parametric form:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[f[1\/2 + I\ t]]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Trying polynomial functions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[
"Consider now a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]random\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
polynomial.  (Trust me, I typed it in at random!)  The unit square is even \
more distorted under such a transformation. "], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := 3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + I\ z - 1; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1}, {0, 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-8\), 1.1}, {\(-4\), 6}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  " But look at the image of the big square going from ",
  StyleBox["-1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["+1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[z_] := 3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + I\ z - 1; \n
    CartesianMap[f, {\(-1\), 1}, {\(-1\), 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-8\), 8}, {\(-10\), 8}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see it raps right around the origin.  This is a ",
  StyleBox["graphical proof ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "that this particular polynomial has a root !  Indeed, a numerical \
calculation of the roots of this polynomial shows that (by luck!) all three \
roots were captured in the square we graphed. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[z]; \nNSolve[3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + I\ z - 1 == 0, z]\), 
    \(\(RootPlot[3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + I\ z - 1 == 0, z, 10, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1\), 1}, {\(-1\), 1}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This example has a germ of an idea for showing that  ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (complex) non-constant polynomial have roots  \[LongDash] which does not \
mean you can ",
  StyleBox["find",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " them other than by approximation.  For cubics there are indeed formulae \
for the roots, but the symbolic form may not be very helpful in relating them \
to something you know.  For example, a combination cube roots and square \
roots results too often in an algebraic mess (at least with this  ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " implementation):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(roots = Solve[3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + I\ z - 1 == 0, z]\), 
    \(N[roots]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  " Part of the problem here is that ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does not try to simplify the square root  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\@\(60264 - 16038\ I\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But, every complex number ",
  StyleBox["has",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a square root.  So, let us see if we can do something about this by \
finding a ",
  StyleBox["formula ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "for the square root of any complex number.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["First, here is the square of a complex number:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[\((a + I\ b)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, if ",
  StyleBox["re + I im ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is any given complex number, we have to solve these equations to find the \
square roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = Solve[{re == a\^2 - b\^2, im == 2\ a\ b}, {a, b}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These formulae are hard to read because the same subexpression comes in \
over and over.  But ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\@\(im\^2 + re\^2\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is just the absolute value of the given number, so we can introduce a \
symbolic name"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = sol /. {\@\(im\^2 + re\^2\) \[Rule] abs}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This looks better.  But, there are ",
  StyleBox["four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " answers, when we want only ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " square toots.  Since, however, the quantity",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-abs\) - re\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " will always be non positive (Why?), we can forget about the first two \
solutions and concentrate on the real solutions."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = Simplify[sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]{3, 4}\[RightDoubleBracket]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Since one pair is just the minus of the other, we need only check \
the validity of the answer for one pair:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[
      \(\((a + I\ b)\)\^2 /. 
          sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\) /. {
          abs \[Rule] \@\(im\^2 + re\^2\)}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And the formula works!  Let's try it in a more condensed form:\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(z = 60264 - 16038\ I; \)\), 
    \(w = \(\@\(Abs[z] - Re[z]\)\ \((Abs[z] + z)\)\)\/\(\@2\ Im[z]\); \n
    Expand[w\^2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We might even be able to make ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " look better by sorting out the real and imaginary parts."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\ w\  = \ \(w\  // \ Simplify\)\  // \ ComplexSimplify\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Since there is a common factor, we can isolate it., ", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[Simplify[w\/\(3\ \@\(\(-372\) + 3\ \@16465\)\)]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So, here below is a pretty good form (with some minor arithmetic \
simplification).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(w = 
      3\ \(\@\(\(-372\) + 3\ \@16465\)\) 
        \((3\ I - 1\/11\ \((124 + \@16465)\))\); Expand[w\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["So, we can put this into the formula for one of the roots.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[z]\), 
    \(\(z /. roots\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]\) /. {
        \@\(60264 - 16038\ I\) \[Rule] w}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Oh well, there are still cube roots to cope with \[LongDash] especially in \
the denominator of a fraction.  And then \[LongDash] even after rationalizing \
the denominator \[LongDash] there is still the problem of finding a good \
expression for the cube root of a complex number.  As this is directly \
related to the problem of the ",
  StyleBox["Trisection of the Angle ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(Why?), there is probably no reason to work at this example further."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Rational functions",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Examining some rational functions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Let us go back to a square grid (taken a bit off center).", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z; \)\), 
    \(\(aa = 
      CartesianMap[f, {0.4, 2}, {0.4, 2}, 
        PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 2.1}, {\(-0.1\), 2.1}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It turns out that the transformation",
  StyleBox[" z \[Rule] 1/z ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "throws the image below the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := 1\/z; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0.4, 2}, {0.4, 2}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 1.5}, {\(-1.5\), 0.1}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So, if we take a conjugate first, it all comes out in the first \
quadrant.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := 1\/Conjugate[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(bb = 
      CartesianMap[f, {0.4, 2}, {0.4, 2}, 
        PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 1.5}, {\(-0.1\), 1.5}}, 
        PlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.006]}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now let us show the original grid and the image relative to the \
unit circle.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[{aa, Graphics[Circle[{0, 0}, 1]], bb}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 2.1}, {\(-1.1\), 2.1}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is easy to check that the transformation ",
  StyleBox["z \[Rule] 1/Conjugate[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " leaves the points of the unit circle fixed, and otherwise it  ",
  StyleBox["inverts",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the inside and the outside of the circle.  What is somewhat remarkable is \
that the curves in the resulting image grid are all ",
  StyleBox["arcs of circles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as we show in the next section."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In general, any  ",
  StyleBox["fractional linear transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "produces a similar picture."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := \(\(-1\) + 3\ z\)\/\(2 - z\); \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {3, 9}, {3, 9}]; \)\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inverting some lines", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
The function that inverts the plane with respect to the unit circle \
is:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(f[z_] := 1\/Conjugate[z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The question to be investegated is: ",
  StyleBox["What are the images of  lines?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The inversion of a line with respect to the unit circle is a circle \
passing through the origin."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  As before, we use some ",
  StyleBox["symbolic computation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "to make the proof.  As the situation of a line and a circle is completely \
symmetric about the perpendicular to the line from the center of the circle, \
",
  StyleBox["without loss of generality,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we can take lines ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  Here are the real and imaginary components of the image of such a \
line passing through the real point  ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ComplexSimplify[1\/Conjugate[a + I\ t]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Do these points lie on a circle?  We have to get an equation in ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to answer this.  We use a method we used before."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(eq = 
      Eliminate[{x == a\/\(a\^2 + t\^2\), y == t\/\(a\^2 + t\^2\)}, t]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can simplify the equation by dividing both sides by  ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((Apart[#\/a]&)\)/@eq\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can check that this equation represents a circle by comparing it \
with a well known standard form.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ExpandAll[\((x - 1\/\(2  a\))\)\^2 + y\^2 == \((1\/\(2  a\))\)\^2]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, we have a circle of radius  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(1\/\(2  a\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " centered at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/\(2 a\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Strictly speaking, we still should argue additionally that the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({a\/\(a\^2 + t\^2\), t\/\(a\^2 + t\^2\)}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " sweep out ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the circle (",
  StyleBox["except",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for the point ",
  StyleBox["{0, 0}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", which requires ",
  StyleBox["t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to become infinite).  But this is easy to do."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{\(-\(x\/a\)\) + x\^2 == \(-y\^2\), x == a\/\(a\^2 + t\^2\), 
        y == t\/\(a\^2 + t\^2\)}, t]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That's it.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Painting surfaces with colors", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " enables us to make three-dimensional plots of surfaces with equations of \
the form  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == f[x, y]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The command       \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Plot3D[f[x, y], {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\ngenerates a three-dimensional plot of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x, y]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " over the specified rectangle as a function of ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  While the command \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(Plot3D[{f[x, y], s[x, y]}, {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\ngenerates a three-dimensional plot in which the height of the surface is \
specified by ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and the shading or color is specified by ",
  StyleBox["s",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Here is an ordinary plot of the first kind."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[Sin[x] + Sin[y], \n\t{x, \(-10\), 10}, {y, \(-10\), 10}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, Mesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The shading seen here is automatically generated as if three \
colored light were shining on the points of the surface from different \
angles.  Here now is a plot where the color is user defined.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[
      Evaluate[{Sin[x] + Sin[y], 
          Hue[1\/4\ \((2 + Sin[x] + Sin[y])\), 1, 1]}], {x, \(-10\), 10}, {y, 
        \(-10\), 10}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 200, Mesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, for a complex function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[x + I\ y]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the height can be thought of as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Abs[f[x + I\ y]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But this is only ",
  StyleBox["part",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the complex value  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == f[x + I\ y]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The rest of the the value is determined by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Arg[f[x + I\ y]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  To ",
  StyleBox["see",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " this value, we need to ",
  StyleBox["paint",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the surface with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(ArgColor[z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which gives a color value whose hue is proportional to the argument of \
the complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Using this scheme, then, here is a picture of the ",
  StyleBox["identity function",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " over the complex numbers."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, 
        \(-3\), 3}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 31]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see a cone, because the height, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Abs[x + I\ y]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", is the same as the distance of the point ",
  StyleBox["{x, y}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " from the origin.  We see ",
  StyleBox["only one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " spectrum, because the identity function makes no change in the argument \
of the complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Here is a picture without the mesh."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, 
        \(-3\), 3}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 31, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Next, we can try the same kind of plot of the squaring \
function.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, 
      \ {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 31]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see a rounder surface with some qualities of a parabola; and we see now \
",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " spectra, because squaring ",
  StyleBox["doubles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the argument.  Here is a related function with ",
  StyleBox["two imaginary roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^2 + 4; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, 
        \(-3\), 3}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 31]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[
"We see two spectra again, and we see a surface that is pushed down to zero \
at two points \[LongDash] but is non zero otherwise.  Next we try the cubing \
function."], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^3; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \ \n
      \tPlotPoints \[Rule] 31]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course we see ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " spectra and a surface of revolution. (Why?)  On to the 4th power:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := z\^4; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 51, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The results are as expected, because we know that  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Abs[z\^n] == Abs[z]\^n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Arg[z\^n] == n\ Arg[z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  And, finally, we try the plot of a polynomial with three roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := 3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + I\ z - 1; \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, \n
      \t{x, \(-1\), 1}, {y, \(-1\), 1}, \n\tBoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \n\t
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 51, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[%, ViewPoint \[Rule] {1.673, \(-1.411\), \(-2.543\)}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The surface is somewhat distorted, but the behavior for large values of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Abs[x + I\ y]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " will be very much like  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Next, we can try some rational functions.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[z_] := \(z + 1\)\/\(z - 1\); \n
    Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, 
        \(-2\), 2}, PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}, {0, 6}}, 
      BoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1.5}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      Mesh \[Rule] False]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The plot is somewhat truncated in order to fit into the box.  At ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " the function is infinite \[LongDash] this is called a ",
  StyleBox["pole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the function.  Here is a view from below."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[%, ViewPoint \[Rule] {1.673, \(-1.411\), \(-2.543\)}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The zero of the function at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == \(-1\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is clearly visible.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
And here is a rational function of higher degree with two poles and \
two zeros.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := \(z\^2 - 1\)\/\(z\^2 + 1\); \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Abs[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, 
        \(-2\), 2}, PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}, {0, 6}}, 
      BoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1.5}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      Mesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[%, ViewPoint \[Rule] {1.673, \(-1.411\), \(-2.543\)}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Sometimes a better idea of a function can be obtained by plotting the real \
and imaginary parts ",
  StyleBox["separately",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := \(z\^2 - 1\)\/\(z\^2 + 1\); \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Re[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, 
        \(-2\), 2}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}, {\(-6\), 6}}, 
      BoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      Mesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := \(z\^2 - 1\)\/\(z\^2 + 1\); \)\), 
    \(\(Plot3D[{Im[f[x + I\ y]], ArgColor[f[x + I\ y]]}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, 
        \(-2\), 2}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}, {\(-6\), 6}}, 
      BoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 3}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      Mesh \[Rule] False]; \)\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Exploring Transcendental Functions (Optional)",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some transcendental functions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The well-known functions  ",
  StyleBox["Sin[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Cos[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Tan[z] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(E\^z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " all have natural definitions for complex arguments.  Here are a few \
pictures about them showing images of grids.  With ",
  StyleBox["Sin ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "something odd is certainly happening arount the image point  1."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := Sin[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, 1, 0.1}, {0, 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 1.5}, {\(-0.1\), 1.3}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := Sin[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, \[Pi], 0.05\ \[Pi]}, {0, \[Pi], 0.05\ \[Pi]}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 15}, {\(-12\), 12}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := Sin[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {0, \[Pi], 0.05\ \[Pi]}, {0, \[Pi], 0.05\ \[Pi]}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.1\), 4}, {\(-2\), 2}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The function ",
  StyleBox["Tan",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " makes nice pictures.  Something odd happens at image points ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", however."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := Tan[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {\(-1\), 1}, {\(-1\), 1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-\(\[Pi]\/2\)\), \[Pi]\/2}, {\(-1.3\), 1.3}}]; 
    \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := Tan[z]; \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[
      f, {\(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi], 0.05\ \[Pi]}, {\(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi], 0.05\ \[Pi]}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-7\), 7}, {\(-7\), 7}}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The exponential function has a well known behavior for reals:\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(E\^Range[0, 3]\), 
    \(N[%]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For ",
  StyleBox["imaginary",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " numbers, there is quite a different phenomenon:  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(E\^\(I\ Range[0, 8]\)\), 
    \(N[%]\), 
    \(\(ArgandPlot[%]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This can be explained by the remarkable formula relating the \
exponential and trigonometric functions.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[ComplexSimplify[E\^\(a + I\ b\)]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The proof of this formula can best be given using the  ",
  StyleBox["powerseries expansions ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of the functions \[LongDash] and that shows the result also holds when ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["b ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are complex.  We do not have time to pursue this here, unfortunately.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is a grid mapping for the exponential.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[z_] := E\^z; \n
    CartesianMap[f, {0, 2, 0.1}, {0, 5, 0.1}, \ \n\t
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-8\), 8}, {\(-8\), 8}}]; \)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The conformal property", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " In drawing the images of polynomials, rational functions and \
transcendental functions of rectangular grids, the curves obtained in the \
images always seemed to intersect ",
  StyleBox["at right angles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "! (Go back to check this out.)  ",
  StyleBox[" Why is this true?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Here is a brief sketch of a proof (which can be considerably \
generalized).  The key idea is that it turns out that the notion of  \
derivative can be extended to complex functions, with many properties of \
ordinary derivatives  \[LongDash] especially ",
  StyleBox["the chain rule",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] provided the functions are \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]nice\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote].   All,  the functions we have been talking about \
are nice.  (Trust me!  However, ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  is an exception.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now a line in the plane has the parametric form  ",
  StyleBox["a + b t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", where ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are complex numbers, and where t is a real parameter.  If  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is one of these nice functions, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f[a + b\ t]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "traces out a curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, taking a deravative with respect to ",
  StyleBox["t ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "we get the answer",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], "[", \(a + b\ t\), "]"}], " ", "b"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Using the definition of dervative, it can be shown easily that this \
complex number represents the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["vector",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve at the point,  and its length is the ",
  StyleBox["speed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  the moving point is travelling at time ,",
  StyleBox["t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note next that ",
  StyleBox[" a + I b t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " represents parametrically a ",
  StyleBox["perpendicular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["line ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "to the first line, and that the two lines intersect  at ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (when ",
  StyleBox["t == 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ").  By differentiation, the tangent vectors to the two curves ",
  StyleBox[" f[a + b t]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["f[a + I b t] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "at",
  StyleBox[" t == 0 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], "[", \(a + b\ t\), "]"}], " ", "b"}], 
        "/.", 
        RowBox[{\({t \[Rule] 0}\), "==", 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]",
                MultilineFunction->None], "[", "a", "]"}], " ", "b"}]}]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]",
                MultilineFunction->None], "[", 
              RowBox[{"a", 
                StyleBox["+",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], \(I\ b\ t\)}], "]"}], " ", "I", " ", 
            "t"}], "/.", 
          RowBox[{\({t \[Rule] 0}\), "==", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                SuperscriptBox["f", "\[Prime]",
                  MultilineFunction->None], "[", "a", "]"}], " ", "I", " ", 
              "b"}]}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",\nrespectively."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These two tangent vectors are clearly perpendicular.  So, the two curves \
intersect at right angles. ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Warning:  The result does not generally hold for functions involving  ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", as can be seen in the next plot.  The problem with the above proof is \
that the properties of differentiation in a complex variable do not work for \
the function ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] partly because it is an orientation-reversing transformation \
and  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  is not."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[z_] := \(z\^2 + I\)\/\(Conjugate[z]\^2 - 3\); \)\), 
    \(\(CartesianMap[f, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, {\(-1\), 1, 0.1}, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-0.7\), 0.3}, {\(-0.75\), 0.25}}]; \)\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Plotting transcendental functions",
  StyleBox[" << Under Revision",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]
}], "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Solving Equations in Complex Numbers",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Existence of roots",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Simple roots", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We noted earlier that every complex number ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has a ",
  StyleBox["square root",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " given by the formula:",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TextForm
      \`\(\@\(Abs[z] - Re[z]\)\ \((Abs[z] + z)\)\)\/\(\@2\ Im[z]\)\)], 
    "Text",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another popular formula is built into ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[\(ComplexSimplify[{z}]\)[\@z]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TextForm
    \`\@Abs[z]\ 
      \((Cos[1\/2\ ArcTan[Re[z], Im[z]]] + I\ Sin[1\/2\ ArcTan[Re[z], Im[z]]])
        \)\)], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In words \[LongDash] as justified by the geometric interpretation of \
complex multiplication \[LongDash] we can say that the square root is found \
by dividing ",
  StyleBox["Arg[z] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["in half",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and by taking the ordinary ",
  StyleBox["(real) square root",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["Abs[z]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This principle is a particular case of the formula for finding (one) \
fractional power of a complex number given in polar form:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((r\ \((Cos[\[Theta]] + I\ Sin[\[Theta]])\))\)\^\(n/m\) \[Rule] \ \ 
      \(r\^\(n/m\)\) Cos[\(n\ \[Theta]\)\/m] + I\ Sin[\(n\ \[Theta]\)\/m]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But remember any non-zero complex number has  ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  square roots. (Just take the minus of one of the formuals above.)  And, \
similarly, any non-zero  complex number has ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distinct ",
  StyleBox["mth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots.  The question is how to choose ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of them."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is where the more precise use of ArcTan[x, y] comes in.  The \
difficulty is that ArcTan[z] is a multiple-valued function, in the sense that \
there are many solutions to the equation z == Tan[w] for a given value of z.  \
Here is the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " function:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["?ArcTan ", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is rather brief.  Keeping in mind the value of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Pi]\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",we have to find what ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does about angles in different quadrants."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(N[\[Pi]\/2]\), 
    \(N[ArcTan[5\/3]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In general when both ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are positive (that is, ",
  StyleBox["{x, y}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is in the first quadrant), then ",
  StyleBox["ArcTan[y/x]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " should be between ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Pi]\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and it should be the same as ",
  StyleBox["ArcTan[x,y]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " 3.0 has built this in:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["ArcTan[3,5]", "Input"],

Cell["Now if we go to the other quadrants, we find:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[\(-3\), 5]\)], "Input"],

Cell["ArcTan[-3,-5]", "Input"],

Cell["ArcTan[3,-5]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So we see that ArcTan[x,y] lies between the limits  \[Pi] and  -\[Pi] .  \
In fact, in the limiting case when ",
  StyleBox["y\[Rule]0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " , we find:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[\(-1\), 0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((N[#1, 20]&)\)[\(-\[Pi]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((N[#1, 20]&)\)[ArcTan[\(-1\), \(-0.00000001\)]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These numbers are explained because ",
  StyleBox["Tan[u] == u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  is  almost true for values of ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  very close to  ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tan[0.00000001]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[0.00000001]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We may assume that ",
  StyleBox["ArcTan[x, y]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " never gets to -\[Pi].  This means that there is a ",
  StyleBox[" discontinuity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " along the negative half of the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  Here are two pictures of the function."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[ArcTan[x, y], {x, \(-8\), 8}, {y, \(-8\), 8}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 30]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[{ArcTan[x, y], ArgColor[x + I\ y]}, {x, \(-8\), 8}, {y, 
        \(-8\), 8}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 50]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These pictures are perhaps a little deceptive, as we have to cut them \
along the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axes to see what happens at ",
  StyleBox["{0,0}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[{ArcTan[x, y], ArgColor[x + I\ y]}, {x, \(-1\), 1}, {y, 0, 4}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 30]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[{ArcTan[x, y], ArgColor[x + I\ y]}, {x, 0, 1}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 30]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There is a singularity at the origin.  And ",
  StyleBox["ArcTan[1, y] == ArcTan[y]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", as can be seen in the picture.  Here are some other special values."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[0, 0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[0, 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[0, \(-1\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[\(-1\), 0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ArcTan[1, 0]\)], "Input"],

Cell["In summary:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Convention. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The value of ",
  StyleBox["ArcTan[x, y]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (provided not both arguments are ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") is the angle that the line through the points ",
  StyleBox["{x,y}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["{0,0}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " makes with the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis with the direction of rotation to sweep out the angle taken as ",
  StyleBox["positive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "for  ",
  StyleBox["y \[GreaterEqual] 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["negative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for ",
  StyleBox["y<0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This also means that the ",
  StyleBox["intuitively correct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " expansion in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " should be:\n         ",
  StyleBox["ComplexExpand[Arg[a + I b]] == ArcTan[a, b]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".\nUnfortunately, the current implementation does the ",
  StyleBox["opposite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This is why we redefined ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ComplexSimplify[Arg[a + I\ b]]\), 
    \(ComplexExpand[ArcTan[a, b]]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["Quadratic Formula",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To review: Take a quadratic equation.  Under what conditions on ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coefficients will the roots be ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, z]; \)\), 
    \(Solve[a\ z\^2 + b\ z + c == 0, z]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The answers are real ",
  StyleBox["unless",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(b\^2 - 4\ a\ c\)]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["negative",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Does the formula work for ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coefficients?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Yes, just substitute in and work it out by algebra."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ z\^2 + b\ z + c /. Solve[a\ z\^2 + b\ z + c == 0, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell["% // Simplify", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course, we needed to know that we choose a square root for a complex \
number ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in the above formulas so that ",
  StyleBox["formally",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ,\n                          ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\((\@u)\)\^2 == u\)]],
  ".  \nBut, as we showed earlier, this is always possible.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Higher degrees", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider the following very simple ",
  StyleBox["cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(F[z_] := z\^3 - 3\ z - 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " solves for the roots of this polynomial as follows."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(rts = Solve[F[z] == 0, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is somewhat messy, but not too bad.  The ordinary reading of these \n\
expressions would ",
  StyleBox["seem",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to indicate that the roots are all complex.  However, here is a picture \
of the three roots."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[F[z] == 0, z, 5, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-1\), 1}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Whoa!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Wait a little minute!  What is going on here?  These computations seem \
to show that all the roots are all ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This seems strange indeed.  Going back to the symbolic representation, \
we may examine the answers numerically."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(N[rts]\), 
    \(N[rts, 30]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The roots are ",
  StyleBox["nearly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " real, but is \"nearly\" good enough?  In order to get symbolic \
expressions that show at face value roots are actually real, we have to \
employ ",
  StyleBox["ComplexSimplify",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[ComplexSimplify, rts, {3}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now it is clear.  They ",
  StyleBox["are",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " real. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is what ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does to a more general cubic equation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \)\), 
    \(Solve[z\^3 + a\ z + b == 0, z]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here are the roots of a ",
  StyleBox["4th",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "-degree equation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[z\^4 + 3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + z + 17 == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["And here are the exact values:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[z\^4 + 3\ z\^3 - z\^2 + z + 17 == 0, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell["% // N", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is what ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does to a more general equation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \)\), 
    \(Solve[z\^4 + a\ z\^2 + b\ z + c == 0, z]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The algebraic expressions do not seem to be very helpful. Nevertheless, \
one can use them to give an ",
  StyleBox["explicit proof",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that every equation of the ",
  StyleBox["4th",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " degree\[LongDash]even with complex coefficients\[LongDash]has a root."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This next example is an animation that shows how the roots of a certain ",
  StyleBox["8th-",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "degree polynomial change as the ",
  StyleBox["constant term",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is varied from negative to positive."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(equation = 
      1\/2 + 2\ x - 2\ x\^2 - 2\ x\^3 + 2\ x\^4 + x\^5 - 2\ x\^6 + x\^8 + 
          c == 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Do[RootPlot[equation, x, 5, \ 
        PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-1.5\), 1.5}}], {c, \(-6\), 6, 
        0.6}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This animation looks best when run ",
  StyleBox["cyclically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  To run it, select the bracket enclosing this section and press  ",
  StyleBox["Command Y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A heuristic proof", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Every non-constant polynomial ",
  StyleBox["p[z] ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "has a complex root."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We give a heuristic proof ",
  StyleBox["by example",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But the style of argument is general and can be mad entirely rigorous."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is an example of a ",
  StyleBox["cubic polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", with complex coefficients. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[p, z]; \np[z_] = z\^3 - I\ z\^2 - z + 2 + 2\ I; \)], "Input"],

Cell["By numerical computation, we see it has three roots.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(RootPlot[p[z] == 0, z]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We are going to use the plot of the images of a ",
  StyleBox["circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the command ",
  StyleBox["ParametricPlot",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to show that roots ",
  StyleBox["must",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " exist.  We define the image of a circle of radius  r as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(curve[p_, r_, ops___] := \n
      ParametricPlot[{Re[p[r\ E\^\(I\ \[Theta]\)]], 
          Im[p[r\ E\^\(I\ \[Theta]\)]]}, \n\t\t{\[Theta], 0, 2\ \[Pi]}, \n\t\t
        AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic, \n\t\tops]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We now plot the images as the radius ",
  StyleBox["decreases",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["0.1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (by steps of  ",
  StyleBox["0.1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ").  (The starting radius ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ws chosen because it is appropriate for this particular polynomial.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Do[
      curve[p, 2 - i, \n\t\t\ \ \ \ \ \ 
        PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-16\), 16}, {\(-16\), 16}}], {i, 0, 1.9, 0.1}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To get the impact of these pictures, one has to imagine the change of \
radius as being done ",
  StyleBox["continuously",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  When the radius is ",
  StyleBox["large",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then the image curve loops around the origin, ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", three times (because the term ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " dominates all the other terms of this polynomial).  When the radius is ",
  StyleBox["very small",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however, the image curve is very close to the constant term of the \
polynomial, which can be assumed to be non zero.  (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By decreasing the input radius continuously, we see there must be (three) \
times when the loops ",
  StyleBox["pass through the origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But the values of the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\ E\^\(I\ \[Theta]\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the circle when this happens must then be the sought-for ",
  StyleBox["roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polynomials in more variables", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Theorem.  Every ",
  StyleBox["non-constant ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "polynomial  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_n]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in several variables has a ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  We are going to argue by ",
  StyleBox["induction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the number of variables.  The Fundamental Theorem gives us the answer \
for  ",
  StyleBox["n == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  But we also need a companion statement:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Theorem.  Every ",
  StyleBox["non-zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_n]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  in several variables has a valuation making it ",
  StyleBox["non",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the case of ",
  StyleBox["one variable",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " when ",
  StyleBox["n == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", write the polynomial in decreasing powers as:\n   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(p[z] == \ \(a\_k\) z\^k + \(a\_\(k - 1\)\) z\^\(k - 1\) + \  ... 
          \) + \(a\_1\) z + a\_0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " , where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(a\_k\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is non zero.\nNow the term  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " dominates, so for ",
  StyleBox["\[LeftBracketingBar]z\[RightBracketingBar]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " large enough, we can show that\n               ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \( | a\_k\ z\^k | 
          \( > \( | 
              \(\(a\_\(k - 1\)\ z\^\(k - 1\) + \) ... \) + a\_1\ z + a\_0 | 
              \)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nHence, for ",
  StyleBox["\[LeftBracketingBar]z\[RightBracketingBar]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " large, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p[z]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot be zero.  (This part of the proof needs to be filled out a little. \
 But, we can see why this is true in the pictures shown earlier in this \
Notebook.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, a non-constant polynomial in ",
  StyleBox["n > 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " variables can be written in the form:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(p[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_n] == \ 
          a\_k[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)] z\_n\^k + \n\t\t
            a\_\(k - 1\)[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)] 
              z\_n\^\(k - 1\) +  ... \) + 
      a\_1[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)] z\_n + \ 
      a\_0[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)]\)],
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_k[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a non-zero polynomial.  (To be more precise, one may have to ",
  StyleBox["permute the variables",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to make sure that the variable called ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(z\_n\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], " "}]]],
  "actually ",
  StyleBox["occurs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the polynomial \[LongDash] and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k > 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash] since otherwise the polynomial is ",
  StyleBox["constant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, after making a subsitution for the variables ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " so that  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_k[z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is non zero, we can choose values of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(z\_n\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], " "}]]],
  " so that either the polynomial is zero or non zero at will.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
